"""In-place ``aten::_foreach_exp_`` implemented with Triton kernels.

``run(self)`` mutates every tensor of the list ``self`` in place with ``exp``
and returns ``None``.  Target-observed ATen semantics (Ascend910B, aclnn):

* accepted dtypes: float32 / float16 / bfloat16; every other dtype raises
  ``RuntimeError`` (integral/bool: "not implemented for DT_*"; float64 and
  complex: "input must be half, float, or bfloat16").
* an empty list raises ``RuntimeError("Tensor list must have at least one
  tensor.")``, a bare tensor raises ``TypeError``.
* empty tensors are a no-op; expanded (overlapping) tensors are written, not
  rejected; a tensor repeated in the list is exponentiated once per occurrence.

Traversal: a tensor whose storage span equals its element count (contiguous, or
dense-for-unary such as a transpose or permute) is walked in memory order, which
visits exactly the same storage locations as logical order, so the element-wise
result is identical.  Gappy views fall back to a linear-logical-index kernel with
per-dimension decomposition.  Both kernels read and write the same addresses.

Numerics: ``exp`` runs in the tensor's own dtype.  The target's vector
exponential handles all three supported dtypes natively, and an explicit
float32 round trip costs two extra vector conversion passes per element (the
16-bit device-bound cases were 20% below the reference bandwidth with it).

Launch policy: a Triton launch costs tens of microseconds on this target, so a
list of small tensors with a shared (dtype, numel) pair is packed into one
2D-grid launch (program id 0 selects the tensor).  Once a tensor's own traffic
is large enough to cover a launch by itself, plain per-tensor launches win,
because they keep the kernel body to a single unbranched tile loop.

Tiles: a tile whose element count divides the tensor exactly takes the
``EVEN`` branch, which issues a static-length DMA with no mask or pad value.
"""

